<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elbląg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Elbing, Elblag</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Elbląg is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula-lagoon">Vistula Lagoon</a>. Founded as Elbing in 1237 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> and settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, it received Lübeck law and became a leading Hanseatic port. It rebelled against the Order and passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a> as part of Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1466, then to Prussia in 1772. Heavily damaged in 1945, it returned to Poland after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elblag.jpg" alt="Elbląg" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sławomir Milejski · CC BY-SA 3.0 pl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SM_Elbl%C4%85g_Ko%C5%9Bci%C3%B3%C5%82_%C5%9Bw_Miko%C5%82aja_(3)_ID_644686.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
